ia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is Your Reaction to the Racist Photo on the Virginia Governor’s Yearbook Page?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2019-02-05T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Engle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Engle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c3fabf1-edc3-5b09-920b-b7fc960df0aa', 'word_count': 1011, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c3fabf1-edc3-5b09-920b-b7fc960df0aa'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether a doctor can fire a cancer patient who wants to use marijuana to treat pain, and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/05/magazine/can-a-doctor-refuse-to-treat-a-patient-who-takes-cannabis.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether a doctor can fire a cancer patient who wants to use marijuana to treat pain, and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A friend was recently diagnosed with multiple myeloma, a cancer of a type of white blood cell called plasma cells. There is often significant pain associated with this condition, and my friend has been treated with high doses of different opioid combinations, in addition to chemotherapy. My friend lives in Colorado, where he has easy access to medical marijuana, and he asked his oncologist if he could add this to his regimen in hopes of decreasing his dependence on opioids. The oncologist told him that he would refuse to treat my friend if he took any form of cannabis. Given his insurance coverage, my friend does not have the option of changing physicians.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/10mag-ethicist-image1/10mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge-v13.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/10mag-ethicist-image1/10mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge-v13.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/10mag-ethicist-image1/10mag-ethicist-image1-jumbo-v13.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/10mag-ethicist-image1/10mag-ethicist-image1-superJumbo-v13.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/10mag-ethicist-image1/10mag-ethicist-image1-thumbStandard-v14.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/10mag-ethicist-image1/10mag-ethicist-image1-thumbStandard-v14.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/10mag-ethicist-image1/10mag-ethicist-image1-thumbLarge-v14.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can a Doctor Refuse to Treat a Patient Who Takes Cannabis?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can a Doctor Refuse to Treat a Patient Who Takes Cannabis?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marijuana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opioids and Opiates', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemotherapy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-05T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fe06373-3f04-5647-a267-a8619c908400', 'word_count': 1411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fe06373-3f04-5647-a267-a8619c908400'}, {'abstract': 'For fitness enthusiasts who also love traveling to far-flung locales, a new breed of travel company wants to take you anywhere you want to go — and give you a great workout when you arrive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/05/travel/get-a-workout-and-a-vacation-at-the-same-time.html', 'snippet': 'For fitness enthusiasts who also love traveling to far-flung locales, a new breed of travel company wants to take you anywhere you want to go — and give you a great workout when you arrive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First came workout boot camps. Then off-the-grid yoga retreats. Next, hotel chains touting celebrity-orchestrated fitness programs. Now, there is another way to travel without leaving your workout behind. Fitness pop-ups in vacation destinations like Capri, Sri Lanka, Goa and Vietnam all offer relaxing getaways combined with exercise options, sightseeing and meals prepared by a professional chef. Often, these groups stay in private, rented villas. These four companies have created custom itineraries with workouts and wellness in mind.', 