': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a19d2cbe-1906-599a-b542-abd4c0443b72', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a19d2cbe-1906-599a-b542-abd4c0443b72'}, {'abstract': 'The Victorian art critic’s ideas about how industrialization stifled creativity and polluted the environment are just as relevant today as they were in the 19th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/05/arts/john-ruskin-bicentennial.html', 'snippet': 'The Victorian art critic’s ideas about how industrialization stifled creativity and polluted the environment are just as relevant today as they were in the 19th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Time hasn’t been kind to the reputation of John Ruskin. Two hundred years after his birth, hardly anyone today remembers Victorian England’s pre-eminent art critic and social philosopher for his books. Instead, his main claim to fame is his ill-starred wedding night, during which unspecified “circumstances” in the “person” of his beautiful young wife, Effie Gray, repelled the religious-minded Ruskin. The marriage was never consummated.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/arts/05RUSKIN-1/05RUSKIN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/05/arts/05RUSKIN-1/05RUSKIN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 464}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/arts/05RUSKIN-1/05RUSKIN-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/arts/05RUSKIN-1/05RUSKIN-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1584, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/arts/05RUSKIN-1/05RUSKIN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/05/arts/05RUSKIN-1/05RUSKIN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/arts/05RUSKIN-1/05RUSKIN-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why John Ruskin, Born 200 Years Ago, Is Having a Comeback', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruskin, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capitalism (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-05T14:48:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Reyburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reyburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/426b7d87-7bee-5287-ae4f-e2ee92a8c8e6', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/426b7d87-7bee-5287-ae4f-e2ee92a8c8e6'}, {'abstract': 'The designer’s official involvement with the storied Italian fashion house began at age 6.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/05/t-magazine/silvia-venturini-fendi-inspiration.html', 'snippet': 'The designer’s official involvement with the storied Italian fashion house began at age 6.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I always say that I was born into a very special yet very peculiar family,” says Silvia Venturini Fendi, whose official involvement with the storied Italian fashion house her grandparents founded in 1925 began at age 6, when she appeared in a Fendi ad wearing a beaver bomber jacket and matching hat. “I didn’t quite understand what my family did for a living, but I knew I just had to be a part of it.” It was Venturini Fendi’s mother and four aunts who hired Karl Lagerfeld as creative director in 1965 and turned the company, which got its start selling umbrellas and leather trunks out of a small pellicceria (or fur shop) on Rome’s Via del Plebiscito, into an international force. Lagerfeld scribbled a sketch of the iconic (and once again ubiquitous) logo of double F’s upon his arrival, and added women’s ready-to-wear just over a decade later. To Venturini Fendi, Lagerfeld has been a lifelong mentor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/t-magazine/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 505, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/05/t-magazine/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 505}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/t-magazine/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 863, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/t-magazine/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1187, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/t-magazine/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/05/t-magazine/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/t-magazine/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ/05tmag-fendi-slide-4HOJ-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Silvia Venturini Fendi’s Playful Sort of Luxury', 'kicker': 'Profile in Style', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fendi SpA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagerfeld, Karl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-05T15:19:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lindsay Talbot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lindsay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Talbot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b77a7591-94af-5d03-b611-6cb0039d9f5a', 'word_count': 1155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b77a7591-94af-5d03-b611-6cb0039d9f5a'}, {'abstract': 'A century ago, the struggle between stars and studios shaped the future of the movies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/05/opinion/hollywood-movies-1919.html', 'snippet': 'A century ago, the struggle between stars and studios shaped the future of the movies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The First World War radically changed the landscape of moviemaking. Before 1914, Europeans had dominated the booming industry — France, Italy, Germany and even Denmark had sent films across the globe. At first they were just shorts, but by 1913 companies were developing long-form storytelling in “feature” films that could run an hour or more. Audiences poured into movie houses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/opinion/05Bordwell-sub/05Bordwell-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 490, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/05/opinion/05Bordwell-sub/05Bordwell-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 490}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/opinion/05Bordwell-sub/05Bordwell-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 837, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/opinion/05Bordwell-sub/05Bordwell-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1379, 'width': 1688, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/05/opinion/05Bordwell-sub/05Bordwell-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/05/opinion/05Bordwell-sub/05Bordw