5e3c-91f8-c1f4ffac80e4'}, {'abstract': 'Hungry for commercial work, the young photographer captured the city’s storefronts and residents with Kodachrome film from 1966 to 1967.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/07/lens/tod-papageorge-dr-blankmans-new-york-kodachrome.html', 'snippet': 'Hungry for commercial work, the young photographer captured the city’s storefronts and residents with Kodachrome film from 1966 to 1967.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tod Papageorge was relatively new to Manhattan and photography when he set out in the mid-1960s to make his mark. While street photographers with black-and-white film darted around pedestrians and traffic, he was attracted to storefronts with their harmonized symmetry and varying shades of gold between jelly jars and cider jugs. Traditionally, black and white was the choice of street photography, and color was for commercial work. His contemporaries, Joel Meyerowitz and Garry Winogrand, urged him to use color.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/07/lens/07Papageorge1/07Papageorge1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/07/lens/07Papageorge1/07Papageorge1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/07/lens/07Papageorge1/07Papageorge1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/07/lens/07Papageorge1/07Papageorge1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/07/lens/07Papageorge1/07Papageorge1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/07/lens/07Papageorge1/07Papageorge1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/07/lens/07Papageorge1/07Papageorge1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Street Photos of 1960s New York in Kodachrome by Tod Papageorge', 'kicker': 'lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Sixties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-07T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Photo', 'section_name': 'Lens', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tod Papageorge and Matthew Sedacca', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tod', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Papageorge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sedacca', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf5b9f79-57cf-5e68-8a6b-90b87024ed84', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf5b9f79-57cf-5e68-8a6b-90b87024ed84'}, {'abstract': 'The library’s dance division — the largest, most eclectic collection of dance materials in the world — turns 75. We picked some rarities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/07/arts/dance/new-york-public-library-dance-division-treasures.html', 'snippet': 'The library’s dance division — the largest, most eclectic collection of dance materials in the world — turns 75. We picked some rarities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those who claim New York to be the world’s dance capital have few grounds better than the dance division of the New York Public Library for the Performing Arts: the largest, most eclectic, and most enterprising collection of dance materials anywhere. It regularly films dance productions in the city, preserving the present for the future; it aims to have a copy of every dance book ever published; it possesses treasures going back centuries. And its doors are open to the public as well as to specialist researchers.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/arts/10NYPL-COMBO1/10NYPL-COMBO1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/10/arts/10NYPL-COMBO1/10NYPL-COMBO1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/arts/10NYPL-COMBO1/10NYPL-COMBO1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/arts/10NYPL-COMBO1/10NYPL-COMBO1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1275, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/arts/10NYPL-COMBO1/10NYPL-COMBO1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/10/arts/10NYPL-COMBO1/10NYPL-COMBO1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 