rganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f7c8cab-803b-5df9-b2aa-9aa330c79792', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f7c8cab-803b-5df9-b2aa-9aa330c79792'}, {'abstract': 'Net income was $255 million in the fourth quarter, the fifth straight with a profit. But the company reported a loss of users for the third consecutive quarter, to 321 million.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/07/technology/twitter-earnings-profit.html', 'snippet': 'Net income was $255 million in the fourth quarter, the fifth straight with a profit. But the company reported a loss of users for the third consecutive quarter, to 321 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — Twitter continues to stabilize as a business.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twitter Continues Its Profit Streak, While Still Shedding Users', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Twitter Use Is Declining, But Revenue Stays Strong', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorsey, Jack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-07T12:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Conger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Conger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89798856-543b-581f-8545-04ba1949a90a', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89798856-543b-581f-8545-04ba1949a90a'}, {'abstract': 'The movie’s plot is as absurdly sensational as its title: The government taps Sam Elliott, an aging Nazi hunter, to kill a yeti.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/07/movies/the-man-who-killed-hitler-then-the-bigfoot-review.html', 'snippet': 'The movie’s plot is as absurdly sensational as its title: The government taps Sam Elliott, an aging Nazi hunter, to kill a yeti.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Man Who Killed Hitler and Then the Bigfoot” revives an exploitation film tradition, in which a sensational title hooks viewers for a movie that’s all windup and no pitch. The writer and director, Robert D. Krzykowski, even appears to have leaned into the concept, turning false advertising into a motif. The man (Sam Elliott), hunting for the Bigfoot, drawls that his average-footed prey is “not really living up to its name.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/08/arts/08manwhokilled/merlin_150234621_04a2b464-2e08-4cb8-b309-6130b365007e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/08/arts/08manwhokilled/merlin_150234621_04a2b464-2e08-4cb8-b309-6130b365007e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/08/arts/08manwhokilled/merlin_150234621_04a2b464-2e08-4cb8-b309-6130b365007e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/08/arts/08manwhokilled/merlin_150234621_04a2b464-2e08-4cb8-b309-6130b365007e-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 981, 'width': 1745, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/08/arts/08manwhokilled/merlin_150234621_04a2b46