to a 21st-century American lyric is no mean trick. Fatimah Asghar’s brilliant offering is a dexterous blend of Old World endurance and New World bravado. Neither human sympathy nor nature’s bounty can fill the void left by her parents’ early deaths; the ferocious melancholy of that single-word refrain circles their absence as if to say: There is no escaping a loss this large — only endurance. Selected by Rita Dove', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/magazine/10mag-poem-1/10mag-poem-1-jumbo-v6.png', 'height': 99, 'width': 294, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/magazine/10mag-poem-1/10mag-poem-1-superJumbo-v6.png', 'height': 99, 'width': 294, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/magazine/10mag-poem-1/10mag-poem-1-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/10/magazine/10mag-poem-1/10mag-poem-1-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/magazine/10mag-poem-1/10mag-poem-1-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: How’d Your Parents Die Again?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-08T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fatimah Asghar and Rita Dove', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fatimah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asghar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dove', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27d8bc5e-555f-5726-a09b-ca2a15b2a8a9', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27d8bc5e-555f-5726-a09b-ca2a15b2a8a9'}, {'abstract': 'We’re supposed to subject our relationships to some recipe for permanent swoon and are made to believe we are failing if we just live in reality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/08/well/family/against-romance-an-un-valentine.html', 'snippet': 'We’re supposed to subject our relationships to some recipe for permanent swoon and are made to believe we are failing if we just live in reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Valentine’s Day many years ago, my spouse, Gil, brought home a bouquet of roses. I am Dutch and he is Israeli, so neither of us had grown up celebrating Valentine’s Day. But it was our first year as graduate students in the United States, and one of his classmates, shocked that he was planning to spend the evening at the library, convinced him that he’d risk losing my love if he didn’t bring me a romantic gift.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/08/well/08ties/merlin_149994186_17bda88d-89c6-45f7-af6e-f2423b0eab4a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/08/well/08ties/merlin_149994186_17bda88d-89c6-45f7-af6e-f2423b0eab4a-articleLarge.jpg', 