iew', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katha Pollitt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3114f976-a01e-526d-a242-40857cbd9991', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3114f976-a01e-526d-a242-40857cbd9991'}, {'abstract': 'In an effort to reduce opioid addiction, doctors are cutting back on pain medication — and sometimes leaving patients to suffer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/09/opinion/sunday/pain-opioids.html', 'snippet': 'In an effort to reduce opioid addiction, doctors are cutting back on pain medication — and sometimes leaving patients to suffer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Katie Tulley suffers from an incurable bladder disorder so painful that it feels “like tearing skin off your arm and pouring acid on it, 24/7,” she said. On scans, the organ looks like an open sore.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10szalavitz/10szalavitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 460, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10szalavitz/10szalavitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 460}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10szalavitz/10szalavitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10szalavitz/10szalavitz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1570, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '