have the same opportunity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/09/opinion/sunday/im-asian-american-affirmative-action-worked-for-me.html', 'snippet': 'Other Asian-American students should have the same opportunity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am a first-generation Korean-American and I’m frustrated by the lawsuit against Harvard, in which the judge will hear closing arguments on Wednesday. The plaintiffs claim that the college should end its very modest consideration of race in admissions. They wrongly assume that Asian-Americans cannot benefit from affirmative action.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10YoungJeanLee1/merlin_81324587_78638e2e-6c21-4e02-b32a-ea235992037a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10YoungJeanLee1/merlin_81324587_78638e2e-6c21-4e02-b32a-ea235992037a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10YoungJeanLee1/merlin_81324587_78638e2e-6c21-4e02-b32a-ea235992037a-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10YoungJeanLee1/merlin_81324587_78638e2e-6c21-4e02-b32a-ea235992037a-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1386, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10YoungJeanLee1/10YoungJeanLee1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10YoungJeanLee1/10YoungJeanLee1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10YoungJeanLee1/10YoungJeanLee1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I’m Asian-American. Affirmative Action Worked for Me.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Affirmative Action Works', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California, Berkeley', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AFFIRMATIVE ACTION', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asian-Americans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-09T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Young Jean Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Young', 'middlename': 'Jean', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/88b04fb8-6e52-5673-b0ca-518ff6a67c01', 'word_count': 941, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88b04fb8-6e52-5673-b0ca-518ff6a67c01'}, {'abstract': 'Despite everything, I cling to the dumb, idealistic conviction that every contradictory truth adds up to create a larger, truer picture of the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/09/opinion/sunday/americas-war-of-stories.html', 'snippet': 'Despite everything, I cling to the dumb, idealistic conviction that every contradictory truth adds up to create a larger, truer picture of the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve lately been having a conversation with friends and fellow writers about the strategic use of stories — stories as propaganda, as weapons. It’s not a question of truth but utility: whether particular stories appropriately conform to or inconveniently contradict certain fashionable, or even ideologically mandatory, narratives.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10kreider/10kreider-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10kreider/10kreider-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10kreider/10kreider-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10kreider/10kreider-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1267, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10kreider/10kreider-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10kreider/10kreider-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10kreider/10kreider-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'America’s War of Stories', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'America’s War of Stories', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-09T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Kreider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kreider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/89f64ddf-a193-53a3-b5ef-0f0fa7ee4e5e', 'word_count': 1323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89f64ddf-a193-53a3-b5ef-0f0fa7ee4e5e'}, {'abstract': 'Stop blaming yourselves. Blame the total lack of social supports.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/09/opinion/sunday/the-real-mommy-war-is-against-the-state.html', 'snippet': 'Stop blaming yourselves. Blame the total lack of social supports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lawyer and I stepped into a windowless conference room in her office building in Washington, D.C., and she reflexively closed the door. I had forgotten to restock my tissues and would soon regret that. By then, I had been interviewing American mothers about their work-family conflict for several weeks. I asked women I had just met what their bosses said to them when they announced a pregnancy, what their parental leave was like, if they could ever work remotely when a child was sick. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10collins/merlin_150378354_6ca68659-bc3b-44c7-a9d1-6b8e7498b47a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10collins/merlin_150378354_6ca68659-bc3b-44c7-a9d1-6b8e7498b47a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 700}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10collins/merlin_150378354_6ca68659-bc3b-44c7-a9d1-6b8e7498b47a-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 878, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10collins/merlin_150378354_6ca68659-bc3b-44c7-a9d1-6b8e7498b47a-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1756, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10collins/10collins-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10collins/10collins-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10collins/10collins-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Real Mommy War Is Against the State', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Mommy War Against the State', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Work-Life Balance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paid Time Off', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-09T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlyn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc8088d5-a19b-57be-b6d7-3ffad3edb599', 'word_count': 1350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc8088d5-a19b-57be-b6d7-3ffad3edb599'}, {'abstract': 'Or mine isn’t, anyway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/09/opinion/sunday/trump-technology-overload.html', 'snippet': 'Or mine isn’t, anyway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am trying to think about when, exactly, I realized that my brain was wretchedly ill suited to the modern world. Was it when Twitter began? With the internet itself?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10senior/merlin_150369069_c70990a5-023a-4374-8952-12c7675ac6a3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10senior/merlin_150369069_c70990a5-023a-4374-8952-12c7675ac6a3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10senior/merlin_150369069_c70990a5-023a-4374-8952-12c7675ac6a3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10senior/merlin_150369069_c70990a5-023a-4374-8952-12c7675ac6a3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1336, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10senior/10senior-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10senior/10senior-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/sunday/10senior/10senior-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Brains Aren’t Designed to Handle the Trump Era', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Our Brains Can’t Handle the Trump Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-09T19:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Senior', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Senior', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc4a02d1-f81c-5de0-8a51-60abf0ab521a', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc4a02d1-f81c-5de0-8a51-60abf0ab521a'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands of chemicals, in billions of dollars worth of products, are being governed by regulations that haven’t been updated in decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/09/opinion/cosmetics-safety-makeup.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands of chemicals, in billions of dollars worth of products, are being governed by regulations that haven’t been updated in decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a 1988 hearing, Congress took the cosmetics industry to task for a rash of health and safety problems. Cosmetologists were reporting serious respiratory and nervous system damage. At least one woman had been permanently disfigured by flammable hair spray. And according to government data, nearly 1,000 toxic chemicals were lurking in countless other personal care products. Cosmetics companies were not doing enough to ensure that these products were safe, and the Food and Drug Administration did not have enough power to adequately police them.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/cosmetics-edt/cosmetics-edt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 558, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/cosmetics-edt/cosmetics-edt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 558}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/cosmetics-edt/cosmetics-edt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 953, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/10/opinion/cosmetics-edt/cosmetics-edt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1905, 'width'