ni Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aee5c0c6-218c-50f7-9d20-df417716d59d', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aee5c0c6-218c-50f7-9d20-df417716d59d'}, {'abstract': 'Two European entrepreneurs want to remove carbon from the air at prices cheap enough to matter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/12/magazine/climeworks-business-climate-change.html', 'snippet': 'Two European entrepreneurs want to remove carbon from the air at prices cheap enough to matter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just over a century ago in Ludwigshafen, Germany, a scientist named Carl Bosch assembled a team of engineers to exploit a new technique in chemistry. A year earlier, another German chemist, Fritz Haber, hit upon a process to pull nitrogen (N) from the air and combine it with hydrogen (H) to produce tiny amounts of ammonia (NH₃). But Haber’s process was delicate, requiring the maintenance of high temperatures and high pressure. Bosch wanted to figure out how to adapt Haber’s discovery for commercial purposes — as we would say today, to “scale it up.” Anyone looking at the state of manufacturing in Europe around 1910, Bosch observed, could see that the task was daunting: The technology simply didn’t exist.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-climeworks-image1/17mag-climeworks-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-climeworks-image1/17mag-climeworks-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-climeworks-image1/17mag-climeworks-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-climeworks-image1/17mag-climeworks-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-climeworks-image1/17mag-climeworks-image1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-climeworks-image1/17mag-climeworks-image1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-climeworks-image1/17mag-climeworks-image1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tiny Swiss Company That Thinks It Can Help Stop Climate Change', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Carbon Chase', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Climeworks AG', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Capture and Sequestration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Engineering and Engineers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Switzerland', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-12T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Gertner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gertner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92fe3246-9f94-540f-b75c-9fef6adb2b7c', 'word_count': 6612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92fe3246-9f94-540f-b75c-9fef6adb2b7c'}, {'abstract': 'Two creative people in two different fields in one wide-ranging conversation. This time: the artist and the architect.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/12/t-magazine/ai-weiwei-frank-gehry-conversation.html', 'snippet': 'Two creative people in two different fields in one wide-ranging conversation. This time: the artist and the architect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2011, Chinese authorities detained the artist Ai Weiwei at a Beijing airport, seizing his passport, laptops and hard drives from his studio and imprisoning him for 81 days without formal charges. The allegation was “economic crimes,” but many saw it as retaliation for his persistent criticism of oppressive government policies, whether investigating the names of the schoolchildren who died in the 2008 Sichuan earthquake or his 2009 documentary surrounding the kangaroo-court trial of the human rights activist Tan Zuoren.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/01/30/t-magazine/oakImage-1548862270268/oakImage-1548862270268-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/01/30/t-magazine/oakImage-1548862270268/oakImage-1548862270268-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/01/30/t-magazine/oakImage-1548862270268/oakImage-1548862270268-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/01/30/t-magazine/oakImage-1548862270268/oakImage-1548862270268-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/01/30/t-magazine/oakImage-1548862270268/oakImage-1548862270268-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/01/30/t-magazine/oakImage-1548862270268/oakImage-1548862270268-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/01/30/t-magazine/oakImage-1548862270268/oakImage-1548862270268-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ai Weiwei and Frank Gehry Talk Art, Legos and Being Cultural Renegades', 'kicker': 'Admiration Society', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ai Weiwei', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gehry, Frank', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-12T10:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jori Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18d06a6f-63c0-5ff5-9f91-02eacabed8fb', 'word_count': 2050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18d06a6f-63c0-5ff5-9f91-02eacabed8fb'}, {'abstract': 'Just as activists and state lawmakers have sought to bring records of decades-old cases to light, police unions have tried to jam the door shut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/12/us/california-police-records.html', 'snippet': 'Just as activists and state lawmakers have sought to bring records of decades-old cases to light, police unions have tried to jam the door shut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — After her son, Eric, was killed by the police in Los Angeles two years ago when officers mistook a water pistol he was holding for a real gun, Valerie Rivera channeled her grief into activism. She joined Black Lives Matter and lobbied the State Legislature to open to the public California’s records on police shootings, which have long been hidden.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/07/us/00calpolice-01-promo/00calpolice-01-promo-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/07/us/00calpolice-01-promo/00calpolice-01-promo-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/07/us/00calpolice-01-promo/00calpolice-01-promo-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'supe