e, Kylian (1998- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neymar', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-12T23:47:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rory Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51e9339c-7c6d-5ae0-8739-2a81c1d13e36', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51e9339c-7c6d-5ae0-8739-2a81c1d13e36'}, {'abstract': 'The doctrine behind MMT was smart but not completely right', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/12/opinion/whats-wrong-with-functional-finance-wonkish.html', 'snippet': 'The doctrine behind MMT was smart but not completely right', 'lead_paragraph': 'Well, it looks as if policy debates over the next couple of years will be at least somewhat affected by the doctrine of Modern Monetary Theory, which some progressives appear to believe means that they don’t need to worry about how to pay for their initiatives. That’s actually wrong even if you set aside concerns about MMT analysis, which is something I’ll write about in a companion piece. But first it seems to me that I need to set out what’s right and what’s wrong about MMT.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/13/opinion/130219krugman1/130219krugman1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 499, 'width': 305, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/13/opinion/130219krugman1/130219krugman1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 499, 'width': 305, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail'