date': '2019-02-14T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth La Ferla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Ferla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a5113f7-3a30-56c0-99b2-ab36e31c6c1f', 'word_count': 846, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a5113f7-3a30-56c0-99b2-ab36e31c6c1f'}, {'abstract': 'Far from the stereotype of total economic isolation, the black market has brought a surprising degree of modernity and consumerism — for some.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/14/magazine/north-korea-black-market-economy.html', 'snippet': 'Far from the stereotype of total economic isolation, the black market has brought a surprising degree of modernity and consumerism — for some.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Night in Pyongyang, around 9 p.m., my North Korean minder, S., and I pulled into the empty parking lot of the Chongryu Restaurant on the quiet banks of the Potong River. It was the spring of 2017, and, though I didn’t know it at the time, I was among the last Americans to visit North Korea before a travel ban took effect. It was my fifth trip in five years to the country, whose supposed impenetrability to American visitors had spurred me to visit as often as possible. On my first three visits, I traveled as an ordinary tourist, which was surprisingly easy to do (albeit in a highly monitored, regimented fashion); then, in the summer of 2016, I enrolled in a monthlong immersive Korean-language program at Kim Hyong-jik University of Education. Now I was back in the country to take an additional two weeks of language classes by day while covertly making further notes for a book in my hotel room at night.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17MagNKorea-1/17MagNKorea-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17MagNKorea-1/17MagNKorea-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17MagNKorea-1/17MagNKorea-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17MagNKorea-1/17MagNKorea-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1252, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17MagNKorea-1/17MagNKorea-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17MagNKorea-1/17MagNKorea-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17MagNKorea-1/17MagNKorea-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shopping in Pyongyang, and Other Adventures in North Korean Capitalism', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shopping in Pyongyang', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black Markets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rationing and Allocation of Resources', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-14T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Travis Jeppesen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Travis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jeppesen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b477e246-590d-5d02-a7c2-2c49e0130eba', 'word_count': 5261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b477e246-590d-5d02-a7c2-2c49e0130eba'}, {'abstract': 'A childhood betrayal complicated by cultural dynamics and learned behavior.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/14/magazine/poem-surrogate-twin.html', 'snippet': 'A childhood betrayal complicated by cultural dynamics and learned behavior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Childhood betrayals: We’ve all experienced them, then more or less tucked them away. Merely reading the list of affinities shared by the two preschool B.F.F.s in Ines Rivera’s poem seems a portent of their inevitable falling out. But this unnerving incident is no ordinary treachery; it’s complicated by cultural dynamics and learned behavior induced by perceptions of, or attitudes toward, gender and race — concepts that are often confusing to children, who tend to take their lead from their elders. And thus fear breeds distrust and spirals into manufactured hate, until another song of innocence skids to a shuddering stop. Selected by Rita Dove', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 228, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 228}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 1479, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: Surrogate Twin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-14T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ines P. Rivera Prosdocimi and Rita Dove', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ines', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Rivera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': ''