ame': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ceylan Yeginsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ceylan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yeginsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80e2f54d-f0e7-540e-8417-2a55c25d9f20', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80e2f54d-f0e7-540e-8417-2a55c25d9f20'}, {'abstract': 'To restore democracy in the Latin American nation, foreign powers need to step aside and let the regional stakeholders resolve the crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/14/opinion/felipe-gonzalez-venezuelan.html', 'snippet': 'To restore democracy in the Latin American nation, foreign powers need to step aside and let the regional stakeholders resolve the crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — Venezuela has been part of my life, both politically and personally, for over four decades. I was a friend of Rómulo Betancourt, the founding father of Venezuelan democracy; of Carlos Andrés Pérez, who governed Venezuela during two terms; and of all the country’s democratically elected presidents. My bond to the country was so strong and close that, following the failed coup attempt against President Hugo Chávez in 2002, the United Nations secretary general, Kofi Annan, asked me to be his personal representative in Venezuela. As expected, Mr. Chávez rejected the appointment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/14/opinion/14Gonzalez/merlin_150095703_e808da91-7986-4f74-a344-611c04019251-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/14/opinion/14Gonzalez/merlin_150095703_e808da91-7986-4f74-a344-611c04019251-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': 