': 'Bezos, Jeffrey P', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-15T16:06:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kara Swisher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swisher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/386480db-d8a9-5030-93bd-fd4c1c829172', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/386480db-d8a9-5030-93bd-fd4c1c829172'}, {'abstract': 'Working at a magazine is “e pluribus unum” at its finest, creative people attempting to solve a riddle together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/15/t-magazine/womens-fashion-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Working at a magazine is “e pluribus unum” at its finest, creative people attempting to solve a riddle together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of the many delights working at a magazine offers, one is the opportunity to collaborate with people who think differently than you do. The best magazines I’ve worked at are the archetypal American high school remade: You have the newspaper and speech and debate team kids (now the text editors); the drama-club kids (now the fashion department); the skateboarders (now the art directors); and the film society kids (now the photo editors). The result, when it works, is a mostly joyful, if sometimes contentious, collective, a group of people all trying to create something using their particular skills and kinds of intelligence. Magazine staffs adhere to a hierarchy, but too much systemization frustrates the fundamentally communal nature of the organization — it is “e pluribus unum” at its finest, creative people attempting to solve a riddle together.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/01/t-magazine/oakImage-1549041083204/oakImage-1549041083204-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/01/t-magazine/oakImage-1549041083204/oakImage-1549041083204-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/01/t-magazine/oakImage-1549041083204/oakImage-1549041083204-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/01/t-magazine/oakImage-1549041083204/oakImage-1549041083204-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/01/t-magazine/oakImage-1549041083204/oakImage-1549041083204-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/01/t-magazine/oakImage-1549041083204/oakImage-1549041083204-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/01/t-magazine/oakImage-1549041083204/oakImage-1549041083204-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'T’s Women’s Fashion Issue: From Many, One', 'kicker': 'Letter From the Editor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Creativity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-15T16:10:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hanya Yanagihara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yanagihara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/507767eb-73b8-58ed-b940-ddde0f6c525d', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/507767eb-73b8-58ed-b940-ddde0f6c525d'}, {'abstract': 'The highlights of David Marchese’s debut Talk column.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/15/magazine/eight-things-we-learned-from-our-meg-ryan-interview.html', 'snippet': 'The highlights of David Marchese’s debut Talk column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you think of romantic comedies, you probably think about Meg Ryan, who starred in some of the genre’s most beloved iterations — “When Harry Met Sally . . . ” “Sleepless in Seattle” and “You’ve Got Mail” — in the past few decades. After a considerable run as one of America’s sweethearts, Ryan turned to darker, more complex roles, eventually starring in the 2003 erotic thriller “In the Cut,” a critical and box-office flop. Soon after — it seemed — she practically disappeared, retreating from film and stepping into a less public, far happier life. For the debut of our new, extended Talk column, David Marchese spoke with Ryan about the future of romantic comedies, the burden of celebrity and where she has been all this time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-talk-slide-LDOX/17mag-talk-slide-LDOX-articleLarge.png', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-tal