ue': 'Design', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coppola, Sofia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-17T00:42:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alice Newell-Hanson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newell-Hanson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07901058-42af-51ce-ba07-820b025fb9f5', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07901058-42af-51ce-ba07-820b025fb9f5'}, {'abstract': 'Nearly half of the films in competition at this year’s festival were directed by women, and Angela Schanelec of Germany won the award for best director.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/16/arts/synonyms-wins-berlin-film-festival.html', 'snippet': 'Nearly half of the films in competition at this year’s festival were directed by women, and Angela Schanelec of Germany won the award for best director.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — This year’s Golden Bear for best feature film at the Berlin International Film Festival was awarded on Saturday to “Synonyms,” a dryly comic, largely autobiographical drama about a young Israeli trying to reinvent himself in modern-day Paris.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/18/arts/18berlinale-item/merlin_150789996_c3cf3a44-69c3-4560-8222-e53175505623-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/18/arts/18berlinale-item/merlin_150789996_c3cf3a44-69c3-4560-8222-e53175505623-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201