rint on Tuesday, February 19, 2019.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Corrections: February 19, 2019', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2019-02-19T04:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/76f69b97-f8b5-5fae-a43c-620376155399', 'word_count': 74, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76f69b97-f8b5-5fae-a43c-620376155399'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on managing the monetary demands of impecunious relatives, a tax-dodging boss and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/18/magazine/can-i-turn-down-family-requests-for-money.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on managing the monetary demands of impecunious relatives, a tax-dodging boss and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My family splits into two camps: people who have money and people who don’t. We didn’t start in different places; we evolved into them. My father, my brother and I are savers and planners. My sister, my aunt and my mother received the same inheritances, the same educational opportunities and the same career options, but they have spent everything they have and more. My mother and sister each filed for bankruptcy (my mother passed away with more than $1 million in debt).', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/14/magazine/0224Mag-Ethicist-1/0224Mag-Ethicist-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/14/magazine/0224Mag-Ethicist-1/0224Mag-Ethicist-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/14/magazine/0224Mag-Ethicist-1/0224Mag-Ethicist-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/14/magazine/0224Mag-Ethicist-1/0224Mag-Ethicist-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1382, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/14/magazine/0224Mag-Ethicist-1/0224Mag-Ethicist-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/14/magazine/0224Mag-Ethicist-1/0224Mag-Ethicist-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/14/magazine/0224Mag-Ethicist-1/0224Mag-Ethicist-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can I Turn Down Family Requests for Money?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Should I Deal With Family Requests for Money?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Evasion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-19T04:21:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6c8d991-4e9d-53ec-b91c-44c79683e6cb', 'word_count': 1281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6c8d991-4e9d-53ec-b91c-44c79683e6cb'}, {'abstract': 'Start with an eight-footer. Wear a long-sleeve shirt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/18/magazine/how-to-crack-a-whip.html', 'snippet': 'Start with an eight-footer. Wear a long-sleeve shirt.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You can’t be afraid of it; that’s when you hurt yourself,” says Jerome Young, who cracks a 10-foot braided leather bullwhip atop his horse as part of the Buffalo Soldiers of Seattle, a group that performs across the country in the historical garb of the all-black military regiments formed after the Civil War. A whip can inflict serious harm. Don’t lean away or flinch at the gunshot-like popping sound. Wear a long-sleeve shirt to protect your skin. “At some point, you’ll hit yourself,” Young says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/15/magazine/0224Mag-Tip-1/0224Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/15/magazine/0224Mag-Tip-1/0224Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/15/magazine/0224Mag-Tip-1/0224Mag-Tip-1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1004, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/15/magazine/0224Mag-Tip-1/0224Mag-Tip-1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1702, 'width': 1736, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/15/magazine/0224Mag-Tip-1/0224Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/15/magazine/0224Mag-Tip-1/0224Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/15/magazine/0224Mag-Tip-1/0224Mag-Tip-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Crack a Whip', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Crack a Whip', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seattle (Wash)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-19T04:22:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f940275d-1083-5123-9d2b-c61a881cf722', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f940275d-1083-5123-9d2b-c61a881cf722'}, {'abstract': 'A dollhouse is the one domestic space both completely under — and reasonably within — your control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/18/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-dollhouses.html', 'snippet': 'A dollhouse is the one domestic space both completely under — and reasonably within — your control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was 8, I wheedled my parents into buying me a dollhouse: a regal Gothic Revival mansion with a turret, spindly porch posts and hundreds of mock-wood shingles, each the size of a pinkie nail. I recruited my mom, as handy as Bob Vila with a drill and a glue gun, to help me build the wooden frame, assemble the staircases, wire the dewdrop chandeliers and faux candelabra. Once the essentials were in place, I dragged her to dollhouse shows held in windowless suburban convention centers, the kinds of cultish expos where miniaturists from around the world sell shrunken works of art. I chose tiny living-room suites, claw-foot bathtubs and damask curtains. (Then, as now, my tastes veered toward the priggishly Victorian.) Gazing at my scratch-made domestic tableau induced a heady, obsessive euphoria.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/magazine/24mag-LOR-pic/24mag-LOR-pic-articleLarge.png', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/24/magazine/24mag-LOR-pic/24mag-LOR-pic-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/magazine/24mag-LOR-pic/24mag-LOR-pic-jumbo.png', 'height': 745, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/magazine/24mag-LOR-pic/24mag-LOR-pic-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1490, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i