 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montreal (Quebec)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Language', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-19T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Canada', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Bilefsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilefsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1025e283-e735-5534-ac61-607e97d66c71', 'word_count': 1201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1025e283-e735-5534-ac61-607e97d66c71'}, {'abstract': 'One of the most frustrating aspects of renting a vacation home online — perhaps second only to a rental not resembling its photos or description — is what is missing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/19/travel/airbnb-homeaway-missing-items-in-rentals.html', 'snippet': 'One of the most frustrating aspects of renting a vacation home online — perhaps second only to a rental not resembling its photos or description — is what is missing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Chelle Neff and her husband, David Neff, traveled from their home in Austin, Tex., to Dallas to visit family over Christmas, they stayed in a one-bedroom loft they rented through Airbnb. On the first morning they were there, Ms. Neff stepped out of the shower and looked for a towel. “I was walking around dripping, looking around the bathroom,” said Ms. Neff, 41, the owner of a hair salon. But the only towel-like object she found was a small bath mat draped over the side of the tub. So she dried off with that. “Thank God for the bath mat,” she said. When Mr. Neff took his shower later, he used the mat, too. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/travel/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT-articleLarge.gif', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/24/travel/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT-articleLarge.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/travel/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT-jumbo.gif', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/travel/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT-superJumbo.gif', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/travel/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/24/travel/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/travel/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT/24UPDATE-MISSING-ALT-thumbLarge.gif', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'There Are No Towels? The Renter’s Lament', 'kicker': 'Update', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s Missing? Towels and Toilet Paper, for Starters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Airbnb', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HomeAway Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-19T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrea Petersen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petersen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/136c126e-0a6d-54b5-a25a-9b386c2fdf77', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/136c126e-0a6d-54b5-a25a-9b386c2fdf77'}, {'abstract': 'The guidebook covers Cantonese food in Asia, Europe and the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/19/dining/michelin-guide-cantonese-restaurants.html', 'snippet': 'The guidebook covers Cantonese food in Asia, Europe and the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the first time, Michelin has issued a compilation of restaurants according to cuisine. A guide to Cantonese restaurants in Asia, Europe and the United States has just been published. Written in English and Simplified Chinese, it’s organized by cities in the three regions and features 291 restaurants, four of which (all in Asia) have the top three-star ranking. The guide includes 16 places in New York, Chicago and San Francisco, with Congee Village, Dim Sum Go Go, East Harbor Seafood Palace, Golden Unicorn, Great N.Y. Noodletown and Hakkasan listed for New York.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner4/20burner4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner4/20burner4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 872}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner4/20burner4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 705, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner4/20burner4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1410, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner4/20burner4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner4/20burner4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner4/20burner4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michelin Issues Its First Cuisine-Focused Guide', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Dine: Michelin Issues Its First Cuisine-Focused Guide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Michelin Guide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-19T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7695b794-f281-5ce8-ae4c-65db28c07fe2', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7695b794-f281-5ce8-ae4c-65db28c07fe2'}, {'abstract': 'Il Buco Alimentari & Vineria makes focaccia with Sicilian wheat, and tops each one with meats, cheeses or vegetables.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/19/dining/focaccia-il-buco-alimentari-vineria.html', 'snippet': 'Il Buco Alimentari & Vineria makes focaccia with Sicilian wheat, and tops each one with meats, cheeses or vegetables.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Il Buco Alimentari & Vineria in NoHo has a new executive chef, Preston Madson, who was at Barbuto and Freeman’s. And he has taken a new approach to focaccia. Working with Marco Riggi — a purveyor of some ancient strains of triticum wheat in Caltanissetta, Sicily — the restaurant is importing stone-ground organic timilia flour, also known as tumminia, for focaccia. The large rectangular flatbreads, about a foot long, are dark and earthy, yet light in texture. They are sold simply brushed with olive oil and strewn with rosemary, or topped, with ricotta, spinach and anchovies; ricotta and rosy salami; and ricotta with delicate squash and mushrooms, among others. They’re served ($6 to $8) during the aperitivo hours (3:30 to 5:30 p.m.) and also sold to take away, $10 to $15 per pound.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner3/20burner3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner3/20burner3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner3/20burner3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner3/20burner3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1325, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner3/20burner3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner3/20burner3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner3/20burner3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Focacce All Dressed Up for a Party', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Innovate: Focaccia Gets Dressed Up for a Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Focaccia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'NoHo (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Il Buco Alimentari & Vineria (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-19T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7e6a797-a39d-54fe-97e0-c829d6847bb2', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7e6a797-a39d-54fe-97e0-c829d6847bb2'}, {'abstract': 'Botto containers are adjustable and expel the air as they contract to keep leftovers or pantry staples fresher longer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/19/dining/botto-adjustable-storage-container.html', 'snippet': 'Botto containers are adjustable and expel the air as they contract to keep leftovers or pantry staples fresher longer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s something satisfying about having the food you’re putting in a plastic container fit exactly. It’s also practical, allowing less air to reach the food so it keeps better. Botto, an American company, has developed a plastic container that can be adjusted from 16 to 32 ounces to fit the food. And as you press down on the top to shrink the size of the container, air is expelled through the lid. The containers come in clear and black.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner5/20burner5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner5/20burner5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner5/20burner5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner5/20burner5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1370, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/dining/20burner5/20burner5-thumbStandard.jpg'