6:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'subsection_name': 'New York Today', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azi Paybarah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paybarah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d92d96f-f3ff-5818-b84e-ac3976db844d', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d92d96f-f3ff-5818-b84e-ac3976db844d'}, {'abstract': 'The president’s move has already been challenged in court. Win or lose, he is almost certainly forcing an alarming judicial precedent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/19/opinion/why-trumps-emergency-mess-means-danger-for-the-courts.html', 'snippet': 'The president’s move has already been challenged in court. Win or lose, he is almost certainly forcing an alarming judicial precedent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It didn’t take long for a group of Texas landowners to challenge President Trump’s declaration of a national emergency in court.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/19/opinion/19chesneyWeb/merlin_117512024_6020dad8-94f0-4e27-a51f-e55b18e26bb2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/19/opinion/19chesneyWeb/merlin_117512024_6020dad8-94f0-4e27-a51f-e55b18e26bb2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/19/opinion/19chesneyWeb/merlin_117512024_6020dad8-94f0-4e27-a51f-e55b18e26bb2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/19/opi