me': None, 'lastname': 'Chesney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/27dee37b-f634-58d9-bfda-6157cf469e52', 'word_count': 1160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27dee37b-f634-58d9-bfda-6157cf469e52'}, {'abstract': 'What keeps Saudi Arabia and Pakistan together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/19/opinion/pakistan-saudi-arabia-prince-bin-salman.html', 'snippet': 'What keeps Saudi Arabia and Pakistan together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KARACHI, Pakistan  —  A few years ago, when my wife and I decided to name our newborn son Changez — which sounds like “CHAN-GAZE” —  my older sister was livid. “But he was a mass murderer; he killed so many people.” The connection to Genghis (Khan) hadn’t really occurred to us. The name sounded nice, and we had announced it. “What did you have in mind instead?” I asked my sister. She suggested a prophet’s name. I said that the prophet might have had to kill a few people, too. Every ruler in history has had to kill a few people. And that doesn’t stop other people from celebrating them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/opinion/20hanif/merlin_150855240_d5384f97-9fbb-4115-96aa-7810939f3c79-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/20/opinion/20hanif/merlin_150855240_d5384f97-9fbb-4115-96aa-7810939f3c79-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/20/opinion/20hanif/merlin_150855240_d5384f97-9fbb-4115-96aa-7810939f3c79-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't