riginal': 'By Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eeb81cf3-94f6-5465-b5a8-c1545da831eb', 'word_count': 989, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eeb81cf3-94f6-5465-b5a8-c1545da831eb'}, {'abstract': 'Living just two floors apart, the fashion designer Jane Mayle and Jewish Museum director Claudia Gould threw a whimsical and wintery dinner in their West Village rowhouse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/21/t-magazine/jane-mayle-claudia-gould.html', 'snippet': 'Living just two floors apart, the fashion designer Jane Mayle and Jewish Museum director Claudia Gould threw a whimsical and wintery dinner in their West Village rowhouse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fashion designer and former model Jane Mayle has lived on the top floor of an 1860s rowhouse on 12th Street ever since she was a student at Columbia University during the mid ’90s. “I still can’t shake the sense that I’m going to wake the neighbors,” she said on a recent winter evening, wearing a dress of her own design (her Elizabeth Street shop, Mayle, which developed a cult following for her beautiful and eccentric designs, closed its doors in 2008 but she relaunched the label three years ago as Maison Mayle). In 2003, Claudia Gould, the director of the Jewish Museum, moved into the third floor apartment below. A friendship eventually grew out of their proximity and personalities: Gould was struck by Mayle’s impeccable eye and charm; Mayle by Gould’s spirited generosity. After a leak left the building without gas for nearly a year, Gould proposed an unorthodox idea: Why not have a “progressive” dinner party for the building, a course on every floor, to celebrate the return of a functioning stove?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 