 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Diana Athill: ‘The Doyenne of English Book Editors’', 'kicker': 'From Our Archives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Athill, Diana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-22T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25b1a361-1a29-5cde-9761-8a866a327102', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25b1a361-1a29-5cde-9761-8a866a327102'}, {'abstract': '“The first time I saw ‘The Wife’ I felt as if I were watching a home movie I hadn’t known existed.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/22/books/review/meg-wolitzer-the-wife.html', 'snippet': '“The first time I saw ‘The Wife’ I felt as if I were watching a home movie I hadn’t known existed.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m not sure how it is for most novelists, but when I create characters, their faces always look a little blurry to me. Though I come to know who these people are in the important ways — their general physical selves and how it feels to be around them; how their presence in a scene changes the emotional room temperature; who or what they love or desire — still their facial features remain indistinct, as in a dream. This blurriness is never a real problem. It’s not like being unable to, say, find a friend at a crowded restaurant or pick out your own child across a playground.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/books/review/24WOLITZER1/24WOLITZER1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/24/books/review/24WOLITZER1/24WOLITZER1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/books/review/24WOLITZER1/24WOLITZER1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/books/review/24WOLITZER1/24WOLITZER1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1312, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/books/review/24WOLITZER1/24WOLITZER1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/02/24/books/review/24WOLITZER1/24WOLITZER1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/24/books/review/24WOLITZER1/24WOLITZER1-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Joan Castleman, Meet Glenn Close. What Happens When a Book’s Character Comes to Life.', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Page to Screen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major