me': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacklisting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-02-22T22:21:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Abi-Habib', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abi-Habib', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d2d9d4a-61d1-5dcb-96d5-58b077bb46a7', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d2d9d4a-61d1-5dcb-96d5-58b077bb46a7'}, {'abstract': 'A roundup of things our editors — and a few contributors — are excited about in a given week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/22/t-magazine/barbara-stauffacher-solomon-sophie-buhai-hair-editors-picks.html', 'snippet': 'A roundup of things our editors — and a few contributors — are excited about in a given week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1960s, the San Francisco-based graphic designer Barbara Stauffacher Solomon, now 91, brought the Swiss typeface Helvetica to the U.S. Creating playful wall-to-wall paintings — which she called “supergraphics” — with the sans-serif font, Solomon helped shape the visual identity of Sea Ranch, the progressive home-development experiment on the Northern California coast. Drawing inspiration for the community’s logo from the curlicue shapes of rams’ horns and crashing waves, Solomon demonstrated that Modernist graphic design could be minimal without being soulless. This month, SFMoMA will dedicate its third-floor gallery to her meticulously rendered illustrations and splashy supergraphics — which helped to popularize Helvetica across the U.S.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/22/t-magazine/22tmag-tel-slide-1613/