 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/62c9840c-7d7d-5fe9-b9bb-6d646d999c2b', 'word_count': 919, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62c9840c-7d7d-5fe9-b9bb-6d646d999c2b'}, {'abstract': 'Why do black people still feel we have to retain white empathy at the expense of being truly empathetic to ourselves?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/24/opinion/michelle-obama-becoming.html', 'snippet': 'Why do black people still feel we have to retain white empathy at the expense of being truly empathetic to ourselves?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I happened to be finishing Michelle Obama’s memoir, “Becoming,” during her recent surprise appearance at the Grammys. She gave a short speech about music bringing people together — “whether we like country, or rap or rock, music helps us share ourselves,” she said. The timing felt appropriate — it’s Black History Month, and although the Obamas are now ensconced in that history, we are only beginning to truly examine their legacy. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/25/opinion/25kaplanA/25kaplanA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/25/opinion/25kaplanA/25kaplanA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/25/opinion/25kaplanA/25kaplanA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/25/opinion/25kaplanA/25kaplanA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp