section_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Vineyard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vineyard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d21ae97a-f652-5e61-a559-5c4896da9deb', 'word_count': 1848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d21ae97a-f652-5e61-a559-5c4896da9deb'}, {'abstract': 'Images can falsify as well as depict reality; they can mislead as well as inspire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/25/opinion/oscars-2019-diversity.html', 'snippet': 'Images can falsify as well as depict reality; they can mislead as well as inspire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A distinguishing feature of today’s politics is that they seem to turn as much on struggles over representations as over material resources or tangible political power. For many people who are active in politics, especially on the left, a primary issue is how and by whom members of marginalized groups are depicted in film, television, social media and art. It strikes me that there is significantly more public discussion of such matters than, for example, education policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/25/opinion/25Stone2/merlin_151205577_d0aaf242-7f01-4044-9598-1bd434d1ff05-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/25/opinion/25Stone2/merlin_151205577_d0aaf242-7f01-4044-9598-1bd434d1ff05-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/25/opinion/25Stone2/merlin_151205577_d0aaf242-7f01-4044-9598-1bd434d1ff05-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank':