ovies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Kenny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c54fc5f3-7580-5f45-86a8-cfc3fd6112da', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c54fc5f3-7580-5f45-86a8-cfc3fd6112da'}, {'abstract': 'Except when Robert Mapplethorpe’s pictures are on the screen, this film does the seemingly impossible and makes him and Patti Smith, well, kind of boring.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/28/movies/mapplethorpe-review.html', 'snippet': 'Except when Robert Mapplethorpe’s pictures are on the screen, this film does the seemingly impossible and makes him and Patti Smith, well, kind of boring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The statement Robert Mapplethorpe made with his photographs was practically self-evident. Every one of his pictures says “I think this is beautiful.” But the statement is followed by a question: “Don’t you?” Seen more than one at a time, his images create provocative, often startling juxtapositions. Consider the spadix of a white calla lily emerging from the center of the flower, next to, say, a man’s penis hanging outside the unzipped pants of a suit. Mapplethorpe’s question became one of the most controversial in modern art. And one that continued to linger long after his death in 1989, at age 42, from AIDS.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/03/01/arts/01mapplethorpe1/merlin_151112820_7e59e89e-f25c-490a-a7e9-171bc1f5c085-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/03/01/arts/01mapplethorpe1/merlin_151112820_7e59e89e-f25c-490a-a7e9-171bc1f5c085-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargehe